@startuml d1-deployment
' Deployment view — the zones as actually built, with the network policies that
' enforce them. Derived from reference/deploy/manifests: every policy named in
' the legend exists in those files under that name.
'
' c0 shows intent. This shows what actually stops traffic when someone deploys a
' pod in the wrong namespace — the policies ARE the boundary.

skinparam backgroundColor #FDFDFB
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam nodesep 12
skinparam ranksep 22
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #6A6A6A
  FontColor #1A1A1A
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #F4F1E6
  BorderColor #B9B090
  FontSize 11
}
skinparam ArrowColor #4A4A4A
skinparam ArrowFontSize 11

title Deployment view — zones, workloads, and the policies that enforce them

rectangle "**IDENTITY ZONE**\nnamespace: identity-zone" as IZ #EEF3F8 {
  rectangle "**gateway**\nauthenticates the person\nrecords the session\nforwards identity only" as GW
}

rectangle "**MONITOR ZONE**\nnamespace: monitor-zone" as MZ #F7F3E8 {
  rectangle "**monitor**\ninspecting middlebox\nTLS terminated · full capture\ncaptures retained indefinitely" as MON
}

rectangle "**CREDENTIAL ZONE**\nnamespace: credential-zone" as CZ #EDF5EE {
  rectangle "**broker**\nverifies the assertion\nexchanges it for the credential\nissues a capability" as BR
  rectangle "**target stub**\nstands in for the appliance\nduring boundary tests" as ST
}

cloud "**Identity Provider**\nshort-lived, audience-bound tokens" as IDP #FFFFFF
database "**Secret Store**\nlong-lived target credentials" as SS #FFFFFF
node "**Target appliance**\nnative authentication only" as TGT #FFFFFF

GW  -down->  MON : request + **token only**
MON -down->  BR  : forwarded
GW  -right[#8A8A8A,dashed]-> IDP : authenticate\n**by FQDN**
BR  -right[#2E7D32]-> SS : **authorises the OPERATOR,**\n**not the workload**
BR  -down[#2E7D32]-> TGT : native login\nusing the credential

note right of SS #FFF9E8
  Reached **without traversing the monitor**.

  If the broker crossed the boundary to reach
  the store, the credential would cross back the
  other way and the design is defeated. Moving
  the broker is not sufficient on its own.
end note

legend bottom
|= zone |= policy |= what it enforces |
| identity | **identity-ingress** | operators connect from outside the cluster |
| identity | **identity-egress-via-monitor-only** | the only in-cluster egress is to monitor-zone |
| identity | **identity-egress-idp-by-fqdn** (Cilium) | IdP reachable **by name, not address** — it shares a host with the secret store, so an IP rule would open both. Name-based keeps gateway → store denied |
| monitor | **monitor-ingress-from-identity-only** | nothing but the identity zone may reach it |
| monitor | **monitor-egress** | forwards to the credential zone |
| credential | **default-deny-ingress** | deny first, then allow explicitly |
| credential | **allow-portal-from-monitor-namespace** | the broker is reachable only via the monitor |
| credential | **allow-stub-from-portal** | broker → target only |
| credential | **credential-egress** | egress limited to DNS, the secret store, and the target |
endlegend

@enduml
